    {
      "id": "mlshpje",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Chemical Properties of Metals"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "7gv3ks0",
      "content": "• Metals react with oxygen to form metal oxides."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0exucu5",
      "content": "• Metals react with water to produce hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "21y0xko",
      "content": "• Metals react with acids to produce hydrogen gas."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "q7omr2v",
      "content": "• Metals lose electrons to form positive ions."
    },
    {
      "id": "pmq7zal",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Non-metals"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cohwjit",
      "content": "• Non-metals are elements that are generally dull and poor conductors of heat and electricity."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a1u8g10",
      "content": "• They may exist in solid, liquid or gaseous state."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6r6r3y3",
      "content": "• Examples: Oxygen, Nitrogen, Carbon, Sulfur."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Metals are electropositive elements that lose electrons to form positive ions, whereas non-metals are electronegative elements that gain electrons.",
      "id": "8u4jtaj"
    },
    {
